                "content": {
                    "content_type": "text",
                    "parts": [
                        "As a U.S. citizen, you have the absolute right to return to the United States, so you won't be denied entry. However, there are a few things to keep in mind:  \n\n1. **Customs and Immigration Scrutiny** \u2013 When re-entering the U.S., you must present your valid U.S. passport. If your Greek residency status comes up, a Customs and Border Protection (CBP) officer might ask about your time abroad. Be prepared to explain that you obtained residency but have remained a U.S. citizen.  \n\n2. **Residency vs. Citizenship** \u2013 Having Greek residency does not affect your U.S. citizenship. The U.S. allows its citizens to have foreign residencies or even dual citizenship without issue.  \n\n3. **Tax Considerations** \u2013 If you have lived abroad for an extended period, you might be subject to U.S. tax obligations (such as filing an annual tax return and possibly reporting foreign bank accounts under FATCA or FBAR requirements).  \n\n4. **Length of Absence** \u2013 If you were outside the U.S. for an extended period, CBP might ask whether you still maintain ties to the U.S., but as long as you are a citizen, they cannot deny you entry.  \n\nIn short, you should be fine returning home. Just ensure your U.S. passport is valid and be ready to answer any routine questions about your trip. Let me know if you need further clarification!"
                    ]
                },
